# The one-way doors: changes here are hard or impossible to undo once
# databases, replicas or clients depend on them (the on-disk format, the
# shard protocol, replication, authentication, and what people install).
# A pull request that touches one ticks "Yes" in the template's one-way-door
# box (.github/pull_request_template.md), names the door, and waits for a
# human review.
#
# Each section below starts with a line `# door: <name>`, and every path up
# to the next such line belongs to that door. The check of the pull
# request's box (.github/scripts/door_check.py, workflow One-way doors)
# reads this file: a diff that touches a door's path with "No one-way door"
# ticked fails it. The last section, `# not a door: ...`, is owned without
# being a door.
#
# GitHub requests a review from the owners below; it enforces that review
# only when the branch rule for main turns on "Require review from Code
# Owners".

# door: on-disk format
# The manifest, write-ahead log, pages, directory layout, segments, the
# fixtures that pin each format version, and the features a file declares
# (query/ir.rs). The stored layouts of rollups, the substring index and the
# analyzers' names a schema keeps (Analyzer::ALL), and the layouts of
# archives (STRARC01) and tenants.
/chloedb/src/storage/manifest.rs   @rkonow
/chloedb/src/storage/wal.rs        @rkonow
/chloedb/src/storage/pager.rs      @rkonow
/chloedb/src/storage/dir.rs        @rkonow
/chloedb/src/storage/ship.rs       @rkonow
/chloedb/src/storage/arc.rs        @rkonow
/chloedb/src/segment/              @rkonow
/chloedb/src/substring/            @rkonow
